findings.body:
**TLS verification is disabled for all environments, not only staging.**

`NewClient()` always builds a transport with `InsecureSkipVerify: true`. Any caller — staging or production — will accept any certificate (or no valid chain), so an on-path attacker can MITM ledger traffic and read or alter responses.

A comment and `TICKET-77` do not gate runtime behavior. Gate the insecure path on an explicit staging-only config that defaults to verify-on, or better: load the staging CA into `tls.Config.RootCAs` and leave verification enabled.

```go
// example: only skip verify when explicitly configured for staging
tlsCfg := &tls.Config{MinVersion: tls.VersionTLS12}
if cfg.AllowInsecureTLS { // must default false; prod must not set this
    tlsCfg.InsecureSkipVerify = true
}
return &http.Client{
    Timeout: 10 * time.Second,
    Transport: &http.Transport{TLSClientConfig: tlsCfg},
}
```
